Louisiana is a startling place to be you  know firstly it's the sort of complex  perversity of some of the locations in  the and the plantations are that you're  struck by this extraordinary beauty of  these places and you're aware of what  has happened and what is occurred there  there's this you know the woodland areas  the Bayou obviously are stunning to look  at and you know and then and then the  life is that there's this the kind of  heat you know our first day of shooting  was 108 degrees high humidity picking  cotton out in the plantation on the  apps' plantation and and suddenly that  sense that you know you're really kind  of deep within this world you know  you're deep you've slipped down the  rabbit hole somehow and and then you're  struck by the kind of reality of this  thing it's a very raw environment it's  the environment is already emotional and  then obviously what is occurring there  is adds another level to that so it's I  don't know I think I went back you know  you know when you go to the book and and  Solomon northup's book and he talks  about the environment at length and and  and only when you're there really do you  really connect in a in a way in a  different way in a deeper way somehow to  to what he's to what he experienced you  know from very early on in the  discussions we we didn't want it to look  like a documentary we didn't want it to  you know we wanted it to look real but  there was no point in there making it  gritty and also it was felt that this  this beauty would be a fantastic  counterpoint to the horror and we were  lucky the locations were you know they  were beautiful and one of the things  that Steve and I discussed from the  beginning was a very painterly approach  to the film and you know  been choosing the aspect ratio we chose  you know usually the two four of that  widescreen gives you an amazing palette  for a composition and the choice of the  locations the amazing work by Adam  Stockhausen the designer you know all of  those things go together and you cannot  help but make it beautiful because it  was the most horrific things happen the  most beautiful places that was the thing  that sort of that was constant for me  and what was interesting about these  plantations was that the sort of you  know the left to the elements and not  some of them are not particularly looked  after or they should be some kind of  natural trust but filming those real  locations you you you you felt the  presence of the past we were dancing  with ghosts when you're shooting there  when you first come on to a plantation  and I'm saying african-american woman  but also my Caucasian girlfriends that  would be in the same pictures as an  American you you start to come down the  road and you just get full of something  you get emotional even if you're not  thinking about it something happens and  I think it is it is the history not in  terms of the recorded occurrence but  it's in it's in our cultural DNA and you  feel it and Steve you said ghost it is  it's a communal experience and it is  things are passed on to you whether you  know them or not and so you feel that  it's very emotional you weep you don't  think you can do it and you have to  really shut your mind off probably the  same way that people that had to live  and had to to love and to keep going had  to just keep going and you're just  you're there just for a while  I think the job of an artist is we feel  that a lot more you see a lot more Sean  than even I see when I walk in there but  your job your discipline kicks in and  you realize that that the point is you  have to honor that  you have to channel that by being as  truthful and as specific and that  actually there's a thing that feeds you  and it then it becomes a trance a  transformative thing where it is sacred  and it is joyful in a way and you feel  you feel connected I went to drama  school to stretch myself to try and just  to make myself more elastic so that I  could be a better actor and that was  what I went through for three years they  threw me into all sorts of uncomfortable  places so when I received the script and  was auditioning for Patsy it was a dream  come true you know a character that  asked me to be joyful and completely  sorrowful at the same time and to do it  in such a way that those things weren't  at odds about a week before we started  shooting I was always you know I when  I'm working on a character I'm always  trying to lift it off the page and make  understand the person further than what  the just is on the page and so I was  daydreaming in my hotel room about what  Patsy would do in the little three time  she had and the fact that she picked 500  pounds of cotton a day struck me as  someone who was obviously had very  nimble fingers and was probably artistic  and so I was thinking about that and I  knew that Epps grew corn on his  plantation so I was like Oh what if she  made some something out of the corn husk  and so I quickly looked it up online and  it was historically accurate that slaves  did make crafts from corn husk and so I  the next day I had a rehearsal with  Steve and we were just talking about the  character and I I shared it with him and  he loved the idea and got the art  department to supply me with corn husk  that very day and you know I I wish I do  feel like it was Patsy's guidance to to  that revelation because it really did  kind of come from nowhere  yeah it is a large book and the in  approach I think all of us probably  wanted to be reductive and not additive  and not try to create things or add  something from a 2013 mindset but really  be faithful to the material and I would  encourage everyone to read it because it  really is one of the most phenomenal  documents that I've had the opportunity  to read I wouldn't be the first person  to say I was embarrassed and not have  known about it prior to that but it has  been a real honor to be part of a group  of people who restored it to a place of  prominence in American literature you  know communication is something that we  certainly take for granted in 2013 but  it was an irony in the book that he  could have written the letter and left  it to his wife and she would have had an  inkling of where he was and what was  going on I also think that it really  spoke to the totality of Solomon someone  used every part of himself his wits as  guy was physicality to survive and to  try to find these ways to create ink and  quill and to steal paper just  fundamental aspects over a long period  of time to survive and let people know  who he was I think was a marvelous  attribute and one that in particular in  the conversations we really wanted to  underscore was what this gentleman was  all about I always felt you know in a  funny way it wasn't a period piece it  was something a conversation we needed  to have now and I work with the music I  wanted I wanted the music to be off from  now so that this conversation would take  place now because I think I don't know I  think Steve and I we work very  instinctively together I mean the  conversations we have about feelings  they're not they're not about history  and and I think within just like music  just like music is you know you can't  really talk about it you can sort of  feel it you can perceive and I think  that's really how the two of us found  our way through this movie some asked me  when was the first time you came in  contact with slavery and I couldn't  remember um any I could remember and it  was a question was like when did you  first know your name I think in a way as  a child as a young  to realize that you know your ancestors  some of your ancestors were slaves and  you was was for me kind of huge thing  yeah well I think I must have been I  mean I remember being very embarrassed  and very ashamed in a way and to start  off life as a person like that I think  it's kind of I never thought of it  before but it must have some sort of  effect on on your outlook on life what  it was about I wanted that every  footprint of Solomon was your the  audience's footprint what he saw you saw  that was that that I just loved that  idea of that first-hand account in the  book but also as I turn the page of the  book I had that same experience  everything he did that I didn't know  what was coming next and I think that  was wanting the audience to be with  Solomon that you were Sullivan I live in  Amsterdam so you know Anne Frank is all  around us  and when I read that book it was like  reading I'm Frank for the first time I  was a kid at school you know it was it  was every page was a revelation every  page was sort of an insight into an  horrendous act will end act whatever in  this time in history in a sense I was  aware of the responsibility of it you  know when I first went Steve first sent  me the script I know you know I just  felt you know there was just a lot of  things in it that I you know I when I  read it I was just stunned by it you  know and I stunned by this story I was  stunned by this character and this time  and I was and I felt the kind of the  responsibility of that the  responsibility not only to Solomon and  to Solomon's descendants in that the  very specific responsibilities but then  also to tell a story from inside the  slave experience in this way was  something that I had never experienced  that I'd never seen he didn't take a  very long time but I realized ultimately  as I went back to the book and I went  back to the script that there was  something about Solomon that I felt I  needed to connect with that actually I  if I could find out about him and  connect to him somehow and I didn't have  to worry about the wider implications of  everything I just had to tell his story  and I  that in the journey of him revealing  himself to me in in the book and and  then the sort of growing appreciation  and understanding that I had for him as  an individual it allowed me to try and  compelled me to try and find him  artistically one of the things that we  tend to resist in terms of subject  matter as Americans is anything placed  in the slave era but the truth is that  we for most of our history for 300 years  of our history were a slave economy and  that's longer than we've been anything  else but we don't want to not only look  at that but we don't want to hear about  a person whether it's Solomon or another  person during that period because of  guilt and shame and anger and all those  things but what happens is you have a  society that that is not embracing or  claiming the majority of its history and  so we get surprised when verdicts go in  particular way and they do polls and  everybody says you think that you think  that it's because it's me it's as if  someone is 21 they've moved to New York  they change their name and deny the fact  they have a childhood  they might rise in the ranks of some  company they might be very important but  they're always gonna be off balance and  they're always gonna trip and not  understand themselves and so one of the  things that I think that 12-years offers  us one of the the great possibilities is  that Steve I feel like you have painted  such a realistic and complex landscape a  reality that we haven't seen before that  there's there's enough people and  there's not circumstances that all of us  could imagine ourselves in it whereas  before we would always be at a distance  and something about imagining ourselves  in it and imagining our people that we  have come from in it makes it real makes  us embrace allows us to embrace it and  so we can see each other's point of view  more readily  we have watched it unfold all of us  together and hopefully there will be a  common language that we can start to to  listen to each other up
 

